{
  "lesson": "03-sandhi",
  "overview": {
    "lead": "Sanskrit words change shape where they touch. Sandhi is the set of rules for those joins — and knowing them is what lets you read a line of verse as words rather than as one long string.",
    "plan": [
      "Three rule lists first, one per kind of join: vowels, consonants, visarga.",
      "Then Saṃyoga asks you to combine two words, and Viccheda to take a joined form apart and name the rule that made it.",
      "Prakriyā is the only place in Abhyāsa that asks for an order: a few derivations have to pass through their stages in sequence, because the grammar forces it."
    ],
    "note": "You do not have to memorise rule names to start. Do the joins first; the names attach themselves after a few rounds.",
    "mentions": [
      "Saṃyoga",
      "Viccheda",
      "Prakriyā"
    ],
    "lists": 6
  },
